 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Anisotropy Regularization
-A variant that removes "needle surgery" structural splitting and training calls, keeping standard densification and pruning [S4, unofficial]. It introduces log-ratio soft regularization parameters: `lambda_aniso` (default 0.001) and `aniso_ratio_max` (default 5) [S4, unofficial].
+Removes needle surgery structural splitting and training calls, keeping only standard densification and pruning. Adds log-ratio soft regularization to control anisotropy. Parameters: `lambda_aniso` = 0.001, `aniso_ratio_max` = 5 [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### GaussianDreamer
-A method combining 2D and 3D diffusion model priors for 3DGS optimization [S5]. It utilizes Shap-E to generate an initial point cloud, which is relatively sparse [S5]. It optimizes 3DGS using 2D SDS. To densify the sparse initial cloud, it proposes noisy point growth and color perturbation [S5]. It incorporates structured noises from multiple viewpoints to alleviate the Janus problem and uses variational 3DGS to mitigate floaters [S5].
+Combines 2D and 3D diffusion model priors for 3DGS optimization. Uses Shap-E to generate the initial point cloud, which is relatively sparse. Optimizes 3DGS using 2D SDS. Proposes noisy point growth and color perturbation to densify the sparse initial cloud [S3].
+
+### MCMC Densification
+Reformulates densification as a Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamic (SGLD) update rule. Adds noise to center locations $\mu$ of Gaussians. Update rule: $\mathcal{G} \leftarrow \mathcal{G} - \lambda_{\text{lr}} \cdot \nabla_{\mathcal{G}} \mathbf{E}_{\mathcal{I}} [\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} (\mathcal{G}; \mathbf{I})] + \lambda_{\text{noise}} \cdot \epsilon$. Parameters: $\lambda_{\text{lr}}$ (learning rate), $\lambda_{\text{noise}}$ (noise scale), $\epsilon$ (noise term) [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Densification is triggered every $N$ steps, making the per-step cost dominated by rasterization and gradient computation. Compaction adds a one-time cost during densification steps [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Pre-allocation of $3\times$ initial Gaussian count ensures no reallocation overhead during densification [S2].
-*   **Performance:** Using opacity decay instead of reset can lead to a significant reduction in the number of splats, improving training speed (e.g., 2.1× faster than standard 3DGS in some implementations) [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Adapts 3DGS to non-standard renderers (inversion-free, ray-splat intersection). Prevents over-densification through decay or visibility pruning.
-*   **Limitations:** View-space gradient scaling is an approximation of screen-space gradients. Requires careful tuning of decay rates and thresholds.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training speedup of 2.1× compared to standard 3DGS [S1]. Rendering is 1.6× faster on average [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires pre-allocated GPU buffers (3x initial count) and compaction overhead [Parent].
+*   **Strengths:** Faster training and rendering than standard 3DGS; handles aliasing better with 3D filters; prevents over-densification via opacity decay [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of tile size (8x8) to avoid divergence; view-space gradient scaling may differ in accuracy from screen-space gradients [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When using inversion-free splat evaluation or ray-splat intersection methods where screen-space gradients are unavailable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Standard 3DGS relies on EWA splatting with screen-space gradients for densification [KKLD23]. As new rendering pipelines emerged (e.g., inversion-free splatting, ray-splat intersection), the lack of screen-space gradients necessitated alternative densification metrics [S2]. Bayesian perspectives reformulated densification as SGLD [S1, unofficial]. Methods like GaussianDreamer extended these strategies to generative tasks with sparse initialization [S5].
+Originated from modifications to the original 3D Gaussian Splatting [KKLD23] to support efficient perspective-correct rendering with hybrid transparency [S1]. The opacity decay strategy was adapted from StopThePop [RSP*24] [S1]. The view-space gradient scaling follows Moenne-Loccoz et al. [MLMP*24] [S1]. The densification changes were adapted from GOF [YSG*24] [S1].
 
